  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "A particle $P$ is traveling along the $x$-axis with displacement $s(t)$ from a fixed origin $O$ given by\n\n$$\ns(t)=(3t-6){e}^{-t},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nFind the time at which the acceleration is zero, and find the value of the velocity at this moment.\n\nThe acceleration is zero at $t=$\n{{field-1}}, and the velocity at this moment is\n$v=$\n{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((3t-6){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-t}+(3t-6)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(9-3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((9-3t){e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(-3){e}^{-t}+(9-3t)(-{e}^{-t}) \\\\  & =(3t-12){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we put $a=0$ to find the time when the acceleration is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3t-12){e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ 3t-12 & =0 \\\\ t & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the acceleration is zero when $t=4.$ To find the velocity at this moment, we substitute this back into $v(t)$ to get\n\n$$\nv(4)=(9-3\\cdot 4){e}^{-4}=-3{e}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is zero at $t=4,$ and the velocity at this moment is $v=-3{e}^{-4}.$",
